<title>ISRAEL: No growth in number of Israeli high tech start-ups.</title>
<headline>No growth in number of Israeli high tech start-ups.</headline>
<dateline>TEL AVIV 1997-01-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>The number of new Israeli high technology companies formed in 1996 remained steady after a sharp decline in the previous year, the Ministry of Industry and Trade said on Sunday.</p>
<p>The ministry's Chief Scientist's Office said 240 start-up companies were formed in 1996, compared with 236 in 1995.</p>
<p>A ministry official said the decline in the number of new start-ups since the figure peaked in 1994 at 291 is cause for concern.</p>
<p>&quot;The decline in 1995 and 1996 is a troubling figure vis-a-vis continued growth in the technology industry, which in the past was 15 to 20 percent,&quot; said ministry director-general Yehoshua Gleitman.</p>
<p>The ministry cited a shortage of technologically skilled workers and a temporary decline in investment capital as the main reasons for the lack of growth.</p>
<p>- Tel Aviv newsroom, 972-3-537-2211</p>
